<div id=3D"Background">
<section id=3D"section-1">
      <h2 id=3D"name-introduction">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-1" class=3D"=
section-number selfRef">1. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#name-introduction" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Introduction</=
a>
      </h2>
<p id=3D"section-1-1">This specification defines a Uniform Resource Name na=
mespace for
   Universally Unique IDentifiers (UUIDs), also known as Globally
   Unique IDentifiers (GUIDs).  A UUID is 128 bits long and
      requires no central registration process.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-e=
ditor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-1-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-1-2">The use of UUIDs is extremely pervasive in computing.=
  They comprise
      the core identifier infrastructure for many operating systems such as
      Microsoft Windows and applications such as the Mozilla Web browser;
      in many cases, they can become exposed in many non-standard ways.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-1-2" class=3D"pi=
lcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-1-3">This specification attempts to standardize that pract=
ice as openly as
      possible and in a way that attempts to benefit the entire Internet.  =
The information
      here is meant to be a concise guide for those wishing to implement
      services using UUIDs either in combination with URNs <span>[<a href=
=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC8141" class=3D"cite xref=
">RFC8141</a>]</span> or otherwise.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rf=
c/rfc9562.html#section-1-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-1-4">There is an ITU-T Recommendation and an ISO/IEC Stand=
ard <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#X667" cla=
ss=3D"cite xref">X667</a>]</span> that are derived from <span>[<a href=3D"h=
ttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC=
4122</a>]</span>.  Both
      sets of specifications have been aligned and are fully technically
      compatible.  Nothing in this document should be construed to override
      the DCE standards that defined UUIDs.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-edito=
r.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-1-4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"motivation">
<section id=3D"section-2">
      <h2 id=3D"name-motivation">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2" class=3D"=
section-number selfRef">2. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#name-motivation" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Motivation</a>
      </h2>
<p id=3D"section-2-1">One of the main reasons for using UUIDs is that no ce=
ntralized
      authority is required to administer them (although two formats may
      leverage optional IEEE 802 Node IDs, others do not).  As a
      result, generation on demand can be completely automated and used for=
 a
      variety of purposes.  The UUID generation algorithm described here
      supports very high allocation rates of 10 million per second per mach=
ine
      or more, if necessary, so that they could even be used as transaction
      IDs.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2-=
1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-2-2">UUIDs are of a fixed size (128 bits), which is reason=
ably small
      compared to other alternatives.  This lends itself well to sorting,
      ordering, and hashing of all sorts; storing in databases; simple
      allocation; and ease of programming in general.<a href=3D"https://www=
.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a><=
/p>
<p id=3D"section-2-3">Since UUIDs are unique and persistent, they make exce=
llent URNs.=20
      The unique ability to generate a new UUID without a
      registration process allows for UUIDs to be one of the URNs with the
      lowest minting cost.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562=
.html#section-2-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<div id=3D"update-motivation">
<section id=3D"section-2.1">
        <h3 id=3D"name-update-motivation">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">2.1. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-update-motivation" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Upd=
ate Motivation</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-2.1-1">Many things have changed in the time since UUIDs we=
re originally
        created.  Modern applications have a need to create and utilize UUI=
Ds
        as the primary identifier for a variety of different items in compl=
ex
        computational systems, including but not limited to database keys,
        file names, machine or system names, and identifiers for event-driv=
en
        transactions.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html=
#section-2.1-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-2.1-2">One area in which UUIDs have gained popularity is d=
atabase keys.
        This stems from the increasingly distributed nature of modern
        applications.  In such cases, "auto-increment" schemes that are oft=
en
        used by databases do not work well: the effort required to
        coordinate sequential numeric identifiers across a network can easi=
ly
        become a burden.  The fact that UUIDs can be used to create unique,
        reasonably short values in distributed systems without requiring
        coordination makes them a good alternative, but UUID versions 1-5,
        which were originally defined by <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-=
editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span=
>, lack
        certain other desirable characteristics, such as:<a href=3D"https:/=
/www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a></p>
<ol start=3D"1" type=3D"1" class=3D"normal type-1" id=3D"section-2.1-3">
   <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.1">UUID versions that are not time ordered, such=
 as UUIDv4 (described in
   <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#uuidv4" class=3D"=
auto internal xref">Section 5.4</a>), have poor database-index locality.  T=
his
   means that new values created in succession are not close to each
   other in the index; thus, they require inserts to be performed at
   random locations.  The resulting negative performance effects on the
   common structures used for this (B-tree and its variants) can be
   dramatic.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-=
2.1-3.1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.2">The 100-nanosecond Gregorian Epoch use=
d in UUIDv1 timestamps (described in
          <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#uuidv1" cl=
ass=3D"auto internal xref">Section 5.1</a>) is uncommon and difficult to
          represent accurately using a standard number format such as that
          described in <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc=
9562.html#IEEE754" class=3D"cite xref">IEEE754</a>]</span>.<a href=3D"https=
://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-3.2" class=3D"pilcrow">=
=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.3">Introspection/parsing is required to o=
rder by time sequence, as
          opposed to being able to perform a simple byte-by-byte comparison=
.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-3.3" cl=
ass=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.4">Privacy and network security issues ar=
ise from using a Media Access Control (MAC)
          address in the node field of UUIDv1.  Exposed MAC addresses
          can be used as an attack surface to locate network interfaces and
          reveal various other information about such machines (minimally, =
the=20
          manufacturer and, potentially, other details). Additionally, with=
 the
          advent of virtual machines and containers, uniqueness of the MAC =
address is
          no longer guaranteed.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#section-2.1-3.4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.5">Many of the implementation details spe=
cified in <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC=
4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span> involved trade-offs that are =
neither possible to
          specify for all applications nor necessary to produce interoperab=
le
          implementations.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562=
.html#section-2.1-3.5" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-3.6">
            <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#R=
FC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span> did not distinguish between=
 the
          requirements for generating a UUID and those for simply storing o=
ne,
          although they are often different.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-edit=
or.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-3.6" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
        </ol>
<p id=3D"section-2.1-4">Due to the aforementioned issues, many widely distr=
ibuted database
        applications and large application vendors have sought to solve the
        problem of creating a better time-based, sortable unique identifier
        for use as a database key. This has led to numerous implementations
        over the past 10+ years solving the same problem in slightly differ=
ent
        ways.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section=
-2.1-4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-2.1-5">While preparing this specification, the following 1=
6 different
        implementations were analyzed for trends in total ID length, bit
        layout, lexical formatting and encoding, timestamp type, timestamp
        format, timestamp accuracy, node format and components, collision
        handling, and multi-timestamp tick generation sequencing:<a href=3D=
"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-5" class=3D"pilcro=
w">=C2=B6</a></p>
<ol start=3D"1" type=3D"1" class=3D"compact type-1" id=3D"section-2.1-6">
   <li id=3D"section-2.1-6.1">
            <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#U=
LID" class=3D"cite xref">ULID</a>]</span><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.=
org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-6.1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-6.2">
            <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#L=
exicalUUID" class=3D"cite xref">LexicalUUID</a>]</span><a href=3D"https://w=
ww.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-2.1-6.2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a>
</li>
          <li id=3D"section-2.1-6.3">
            <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#S=
nowflake" class=3D"cite xref">Snowflake</a>]</span><a href=3D"https://www.r=
