<div id=3D"format">
<section id=3D"section-4">
      <h2 id=3D"name-uuid-format">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4" class=3D"=
section-number selfRef">4. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#name-uuid-format" class=3D"section-name selfRef">UUID Format</a>
      </h2>
<p id=3D"section-4-1">The UUID format is 16 octets (128 bits) in size; the =
variant bits in
      conjunction with the version bits described in the next sections
      determine finer structure. In terms of these UUID formats and layout,=
 bit
      definitions start at 0 and end at 127, while octet definitions start =
at 0
      and end at 15.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#=
section-4-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-4-2">In the absence of explicit application or presentatio=
n protocol
      specification to the contrary, each field is encoded with the most
      significant byte first (known as "network byte order").<a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-4-3">Saving UUIDs to binary format is done by sequencing a=
ll fields in
      big-endian format.  However, there is a known caveat that Microsoft's
      Component Object Model (COM) GUIDs leverage little-endian when saving
      GUIDs.  The discussion of this (see <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc=
-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#MS_COM_GUID" class=3D"cite xref">MS_COM_GUID</=
a>]</span>) is outside
      the scope of this specification.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org=
/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-4-4">UUIDs <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> be represented=
 as binary data or integers.
      When in use with URNs or as text in applications, any given UUID shou=
ld
      be represented by the "hex-and-dash" string format consisting of
      multiple groups of uppercase or lowercase alphanumeric hexadecimal
      characters separated by single dashes/hyphens.  When used with databa=
ses,
      please refer to <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.htm=
l#database_considerations" class=3D"auto internal xref">Section 6.13</a>.<a=
 href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-4" class=3D"=
pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-4-5">The formal definition of the UUID string representati=
on is provided by the following ABNF <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-edit=
or.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC5234" class=3D"cite xref">RFC5234</a>]</span>:<a=
 href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-5" class=3D"=
pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<div class=3D"lang-abnf sourcecode" id=3D"section-4-6">
<pre>UUID     =3D 4hexOctet "-"
           2hexOctet "-"
           2hexOctet "-"
           2hexOctet "-"
           6hexOctet
hexOctet =3D HEXDIG HEXDIG
DIGIT    =3D %x30-39
HEXDIG   =3D DIGIT / "A" / "B" / "C" / "D" / "E" / "F"
</pre><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-6" c=
lass=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</div>
<p id=3D"section-4-7">Note that the alphabetic characters may be all upperc=
ase, all lowercase, or mixed case, as per <span><a href=3D"https://rfc-edit=
or.org/rfc/rfc5234#section-2.3" class=3D"relref">Section 2.3</a> of [<a hre=
f=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC5234" class=3D"cite xre=
f">RFC5234</a>]</span>.
An example UUID using this textual representation from the above ABNF is sh=
own in <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sampleStringU=
UID" class=3D"auto internal xref">Figure 1</a>.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-e=
ditor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-7" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<span id=3D"name-example-string-uuid-format"></span><div id=3D"sampleString=
UUID">
<figure id=3D"figure-1">
        <div class=3D"alignLeft art-text artwork" id=3D"section-4-8.1">
<pre>f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6
</pre>
</div>
<figcaption><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#figure-1=
" class=3D"selfRef">Figure 1</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-example-string-=
uuid-format" class=3D"selfRef">Example String UUID Format</a>
        </figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p id=3D"section-4-9">The same UUID from <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.=
org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sampleStringUUID" class=3D"auto internal xref">Figure =
1</a> is represented in binary (<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/r=
fc9562.html#sampleBinaryUUID" class=3D"auto internal xref">Figure 2</a>), a=
s an unsigned integer (<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.ht=
ml#sampleIntegerUUID" class=3D"auto internal xref">Figure 3</a>), and as a =
URN (<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sampleURNUUID" =
class=3D"auto internal xref">Figure 4</a>) defined by <span>[<a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC8141" class=3D"cite xref">RFC81=
41</a>]</span>.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#secti=
on-4-9" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<span id=3D"name-example-binary-uuid"></span><div id=3D"sampleBinaryUUID">
<figure id=3D"figure-2">
        <div class=3D"alignLeft art-text artwork" id=3D"section-4-10.1">
<pre>111110000001110101001111101011100111110111101100000100011101000\
01010011101100101000000001010000011001001000111100110101111110110
</pre>
</div>
<figcaption><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#figure-2=
" class=3D"selfRef">Figure 2</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-example-binary-=
uuid" class=3D"selfRef">Example Binary UUID</a>
        </figcaption></figure>
</div>
<span id=3D"name-example-unsigned-integer-uu"></span><div id=3D"sampleInteg=
erUUID">
<figure id=3D"figure-3">
        <div class=3D"alignLeft art-text artwork" id=3D"section-4-11.1">
<pre>329800735698586629295641978511506172918
</pre>
</div>
<figcaption><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#figure-3=
" class=3D"selfRef">Figure 3</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-example-unsigne=
d-integer-uu" class=3D"selfRef">Example Unsigned Integer UUID (Shown as a D=
ecimal Number)</a>
        </figcaption></figure>
</div>
<span id=3D"name-example-urn-namespace-for-u"></span><div id=3D"sampleURNUU=
ID">
<figure id=3D"figure-4">
        <div class=3D"alignLeft art-text artwork" id=3D"section-4-12.1">
<pre>urn:uuid:f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6
</pre>
</div>
<figcaption><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#figure-4=
" class=3D"selfRef">Figure 4</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-example-urn-nam=
espace-for-u" class=3D"selfRef">Example URN Namespace for UUID</a>
        </figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p id=3D"section-4-13">There are many other ways to define a UUID format; s=
ome examples are detailed below.
Please note that this is not an exhaustive list and is only provided for in=
formational purposes.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html=
#section-4-13" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<ul class=3D"normal">
<li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-4-14.1">Some UUID implementations, such =
as those found in <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.=
html#Python" class=3D"cite xref">Python</a>]</span> and <span>[<a href=3D"h=
ttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#Microsoft" class=3D"cite xref">M=
icrosoft</a>]</span>, will output UUID
        with the string format, including dashes, enclosed in curly braces.=
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4-14.1" clas=
s=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
        <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-4-14.2">
          <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#X66=
7" class=3D"cite xref">X667</a>]</span> provides UUID format definitions fo=
r use of
        UUID with an OID.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.=
html#section-4-14.2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
        <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-4-14.3">
          <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#IBM=
_NCS" class=3D"cite xref">IBM_NCS</a>]</span> is a legacy implementation th=
at produces a
        unique UUID format compatible with Variant 0xx of <a href=3D"https:=
//www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#table1" class=3D"auto internal xref">=
Table 1</a>.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-=
4-14.3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
      </ul>
<div id=3D"variant_field">
<section id=3D"section-4.1">
        <h3 id=3D"name-variant-field">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4.1" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">4.1. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-variant-field" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Variant=
 Field</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-4.1-1">The variant field determines the layout of the UUID=
.  That is, the
        interpretation of all other bits in the UUID depends on the setting=
 of
        the bits in the variant field.  As such, it could more accurately b=
e
        called a "type" field; we retain the original term for compatibilit=
y.
        The variant field consists of a variable number of the most
        significant bits of octet 8 of the UUID.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-=
editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4.1-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-4.1-2"><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.h=
tml#table1" class=3D"auto internal xref">Table 1</a> lists the contents of =
the variant field,
        where the letter "x" indicates a "don't-care" value.<a href=3D"http=
s://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-4.1-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=
=C2=B6</a></p>
<span id=3D"name-uuid-variants"></span><div id=3D"table1">
<table class=3D"center" id=3D"table-1">
          <caption>
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#table-1" class=3D"se=
lfRef">Table 1</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-uuid-variants" =
class=3D"selfRef">UUID Variants</a>
          </caption>
<thead>
            <tr>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">MSB0</th>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">MSB1</th>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">MSB2</th>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">MSB3</th>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">Variant</=
th>
              <th class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">Descripti=
on</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">0</td>
              <td class=3D"text-left" rowspan=3D"1" colspan=3D"1">x</td>
