<p id=3D"section-6.6-4">Items may be added to this registry using the Speci=
fication Required
        policy as per <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9=
562.html#RFC8126" class=3D"cite xref">RFC8126</a>]</span>.<a href=3D"https:=
//www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.6-5">For designated experts, generally speaking, Namespa=
ce IDs are
        allocated as follows:<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9=
562.html#section-6.6-5" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<ul class=3D"normal">
<li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.6-6.1">The first Namespace ID value, f=
or DNS, was calculated from a
          time-based UUIDv1 and "6ba7b810-9dad-11d1-80b4-00c04fd430c8", use=
d as
          a starting point.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc956=
2.html#section-6.6-6.1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.6-6.2">Subsequent Namespace =
ID values increment the
          least significant, rightmost bit of time_low "6ba7b810" while
          freezing the rest of the UUID to "9dad-11d1-80b4-00c04fd430c8".<a=
 href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-6.2" class=
=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.6-6.3">New Namespace ID valu=
es <span class=3D"bcp14">MUST</span> use this same
          logic and <span class=3D"bcp14">MUST NOT</span> use a previously =
used Namespace ID
          value.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sect=
ion-6.6-6.3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.6-6.4">Thus, "6ba7b815" is t=
he next available time_low for a new
          Namespace ID value with the full ID being
          "6ba7b815-9dad-11d1-80b4-00c04fd430c8".<a href=3D"https://www.rfc=
-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-6.4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.6-6.5">The upper bound for t=
ime_low in this special use, Namespace ID
          values, is "ffffffff" or "ffffffff-9dad-11d1-80b4-00c04fd430c8",
          which should be sufficient space for future Namespace ID values.<=
a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-6.5" clas=
s=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
        </ul>
<p id=3D"section-6.6-7">Note that the Namespace ID value
        "6ba7b813-9dad-11d1-80b4-00c04fd430c8" and its usage are not define=
d by
        this document or by <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rf=
c/rfc9562.html#RFC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span>; thus, it <=
span class=3D"bcp14">SHOULD NOT</span> be used as a Namespace ID value.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-7" class=3D"=
pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.6-8">New Namespace ID values <span class=3D"bcp14">MUST<=
/span> be documented as per
        <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#IANA" class=
=3D"auto internal xref">Section 7</a> if they are to be globally available =
and fully
        interoperable.  Implementations <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> co=
ntinue to use
        vendor-specific, application-specific, and deployment-specific
        Namespace ID values; but know that interoperability is not guarante=
ed.
        These custom Namespace ID values <span class=3D"bcp14">MUST NOT</sp=
an> use the logic
        above; instead, generating a
        UUIDv4 or UUIDv7 Namespace ID value is <span class=3D"bcp14">RECOMM=
ENDED</span>.  If collision probability (<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.=
org/rfc/rfc9562.html#collision_resistance" class=3D"auto internal xref">Sec=
tion 6.7</a>) and uniqueness (<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc=
9562.html#global_local_uniqueness" class=3D"auto internal xref">Section 6.8=
</a>) of the final name-based UUID are
        not a problem, an implementation <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> a=
lso leverage
        UUIDv8 instead to create a custom, application-specific Namespace I=
D
        value.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sectio=
n-6.6-8" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.6-9">Implementations <span class=3D"bcp14">SHOULD</span>=
 provide the ability to input
        a custom namespace to account for newly registered IANA Namespace I=
D
        values outside of those listed in this section or custom,
        application-specific Namespace ID values.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc=
-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.6-9" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p=
>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"collision_resistance">
<section id=3D"section-6.7">
        <h3 id=3D"name-collision-resistance">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.7" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.7. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-collision-resistance" class=3D"section-name selfRef">=
Collision Resistance</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.7-1">Implementations should weigh the consequences of UU=
ID collisions
        within their application and when deciding between UUID versions th=
at
        use entropy (randomness) versus the other components such as those =
in
        Sections <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#tim=
estamp_considerations" class=3D"auto internal xref">6.1</a>
        and <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#monotoni=
city_counters" class=3D"auto internal xref">6.2</a>.  This is
        especially true for distributed node collision resistance as define=
d
        by <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#distribut=
ed_shared_knowledge" class=3D"auto internal xref">Section 6.4</a>.<a href=
=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.7-1" class=3D"pil=
crow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.7-2">There are two example scenarios below that help ill=
ustrate the
        varying seriousness of a collision within an application.<a href=3D=
"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.7-2" class=3D"pilcro=
w">=C2=B6</a></p>
<span class=3D"break"></span><dl class=3D"dlNewline" id=3D"section-6.7-3">
          <dt id=3D"section-6.7-3.1">Low Impact:</dt>
          <dd style=3D"margin-left: 1.5em" id=3D"section-6.7-3.2">A UUID co=
llision generated a duplicate log entry, which results
          in incorrect statistics derived from the data. Implementations th=
at
          are not negatively affected by collisions may continue with the
          entropy and uniqueness provided by UUIDs defined in this document=
.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.7-3.2" cl=
ass=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</dd>
          <dd class=3D"break"></dd>
<dt id=3D"section-6.7-3.3">High Impact:</dt>
          <dd style=3D"margin-left: 1.5em" id=3D"section-6.7-3.4">A duplica=
te key causes an airplane to receive the wrong course,
          which puts people's lives at risk. In this scenario, there is no
          margin for error. Collisions must be avoided: failure is
          unacceptable. Applications dealing with this type of scenario mus=
t
          employ as much collision resistance as possible within the given
          application context.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc=
9562.html#section-6.7-3.4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</dd>
        <dd class=3D"break"></dd>
</dl>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"global_local_uniqueness">
<section id=3D"section-6.8">
        <h3 id=3D"name-global-and-local-uniqueness">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.8" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.8. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-global-and-local-uniqueness" class=3D"section-name se=
lfRef">Global and Local Uniqueness</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.8-1">UUIDs created by this specification <span class=3D"=
bcp14">MAY</span> be used to
        provide local uniqueness guarantees.  For example, ensuring UUIDs
        created within a local application context are unique within a
        database <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> be sufficient for some im=
plementations
        where global uniqueness outside of the application context, in othe=
r
        applications, or around the world is not required.<a href=3D"https:=
//www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.8-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.8-2">Although true global uniqueness is impossible to gu=
arantee without
        a shared knowledge scheme, a shared knowledge scheme is not require=
d
        by a UUID to provide uniqueness for practical implementation purpos=
es.
        Implementations <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> use a shared knowl=
edge
        scheme, introduced in <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc=
9562.html#distributed_shared_knowledge" class=3D"auto internal xref">Sectio=
n 6.4</a>,
        as they see fit to extend the uniqueness guaranteed by this
        specification.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.htm=
l#section-6.8-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"unguessability">
<section id=3D"section-6.9">
        <h3 id=3D"name-unguessability">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.9" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.9. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-unguessability" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Ungues=
sability</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.9-1">Implementations <span class=3D"bcp14">SHOULD</span>=
 utilize a cryptographically
        secure pseudorandom number generator (CSPRNG) to provide values tha=
t
        are both difficult to predict ("unguessable") and have a low
        likelihood of collision ("unique").  The exception is when a suitab=
le
        CSPRNG is unavailable in the execution environment.  Take care to
        ensure the CSPRNG state is properly reseeded upon state changes, su=
ch
        as process forks, to ensure proper CSPRNG operation.  CSPRNG ensure=
s
        the best of Sections <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9=
562.html#collision_resistance" class=3D"auto internal xref">6.7</a> and <a =
href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#Security" class=3D"auto=
 internal xref">8</a> are
        present in modern UUIDs.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/r=
fc9562.html#section-6.9-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.9-2">Further advice on generating cryptographic-quality =
random numbers
        can be found in <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#RFC4086" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4086</a>]</span>, <span>[<a href=
=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC8937" class=3D"cite xref=
">RFC8937</a>]</span>,
        and <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#R=
ANDOM" class=3D"cite xref">RANDOM</a>]</span>.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-ed=
itor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.9-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"unidentifiable">
<section id=3D"section-6.10">
        <h3 id=3D"name-uuids-that-do-not-identify-">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.10. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org=
/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-uuids-that-do-not-identify-" class=3D"section-name s=
elfRef">UUIDs That Do Not Identify the Host</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-1">This section describes how to generate a UUIDv1 or=
 UUIDv6 value if
        an IEEE 802 address is not available or its use is not desired.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10-1" class=3D=
"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-2">Implementations <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY</span> l=
everage MAC address
        randomization techniques <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.o=
rg/rfc/rfc9562.html#IEEE802.11bh" class=3D"cite xref">IEEE802.11bh</a>]</sp=
an> as an alternative to the pseudorandom logic
        provided in this section.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/=
rfc9562.html#section-6.10-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-3">Alternatively, implementations <span class=3D"bcp1=
4">MAY</span> elect to obtain a
        48-bit cryptographic-quality random number as per <a href=3D"https:=
//www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#unguessability" class=3D"auto interna=
l xref">Section 6.9</a> to use as the Node ID.  After generating the
        48-bit fully randomized node value, implementations
        <span class=3D"bcp14">MUST</span> set the least significant bit of =
the first octet
        of the Node ID to 1.  This bit is the unicast or multicast bit, whi=
ch
        will never be set in IEEE 802 addresses obtained from network cards=
.
        Hence, there can never be a conflict between UUIDs generated by
        machines with and without network cards.  An example of generating =
a
        randomized 48-bit node value and the subsequent bit modification is
        detailed in <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#=
test_vectors" class=3D"auto internal xref">Appendix A</a>.  For more inform=
ation about
        IEEE 802 address and the unicast or multicast or local/global bits,
        please review <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9=
562.html#RFC9542" class=3D"cite xref">RFC9542</a>]</span>.<a href=3D"https:=
//www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=
=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-4">For compatibility with earlier specifications, not=
e that this
        document uses the unicast or multicast bit instead of the arguably =
more
        correct local/global bit because MAC addresses with the local/globa=
l
        bit set or not set are both possible in a network.  This is not the=
 case
        with the unicast or multicast bit.  One node cannot have a MAC addr=
ess
        that multicasts to multiple nodes.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor=
.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10-4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-5">In addition, items such as the computer's name and=
 the name of the
        operating system, while not strictly speaking random, will help
        differentiate the results from those obtained by other systems.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10-5" class=3D=
"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.10-6">The exact algorithm to generate a Node ID using th=
ese data is
        system specific because both the data available and the functions t=
o
        obtain them are often very system specific.  However, a generic app=
roach
        is to accumulate as many sources as possible into a buffer, use a
        message digest (such as SHA-256 or SHA-512 defined by <span>[<a hre=
f=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#FIPS180-4" class=3D"cite x=
ref">FIPS180-4</a>]</span>), take an arbitrary 6 bytes from the hash value,
        and set the multicast bit as described above.<a href=3D"https://www=
.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.10-6" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</=
a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"sorting">
<section id=3D"section-6.11">
        <h3 id=3D"name-sorting">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.11" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.11. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org=
/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-sorting" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Sorting</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.11-1">UUIDv6 and UUIDv7 are designed so that implementat=
ions that require
        sorting (e.g., database indexes) sort as opaque raw bytes without t=
he
        need for parsing or introspection.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor=
.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.11-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.11-2">Time-ordered monotonic UUIDs benefit from greater =
database-index
        locality because the new values are near each other in the index.  =
As
        a result, objects are more easily clustered together for better
        performance.  The real-world differences in this approach of index
        locality versus random data inserts can be one order of magnitude o=
r
        more.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section=
-6.11-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.11-3">UUID formats created by this specification are int=
ended to be
        lexicographically sortable while in the textual representation.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.11-3" class=3D=
"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.11-4">UUIDs created by this specification are crafted wi=
th big-endian
        byte order (network byte order) in mind. If little-endian style is
        required, UUIDv8 is available for custom UUID formats.<a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.11-4" class=3D"pilcrow"=
>=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"opacity">
<section id=3D"section-6.12">
        <h3 id=3D"name-opacity">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.12" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.12. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org=
/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-opacity" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Opacity</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.12-1">As general guidance, avoiding parsing UUID values
        unnecessarily is recommended; instead, treat UUIDs as opaquely as p=
ossible.
        Although application-specific concerns could, of course, require so=
me
        degree of introspection (e.g., to examine Sections <a href=3D"https=
://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#variant_field" class=3D"auto interna=
l xref">4.1</a> or <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#v=
ersion_field" class=3D"auto internal xref">4.2</a> or perhaps the timestamp=
 of
        a UUID), the advice here is to avoid this or other parsing unless
        absolutely necessary.  Applications typically tend to be simpler, b=
e more
        interoperable, and perform better when this advice is followed.<a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.12-1" class=3D=
"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"database_considerations">
<section id=3D"section-6.13">
        <h3 id=3D"name-dbms-and-database-considera">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.13" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">6.13. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org=
/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-dbms-and-database-considera" class=3D"section-name s=
elfRef">DBMS and Database Considerations</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-6.13-1">For many applications, such as databases, storing =
UUIDs as text is
        unnecessarily verbose, requiring 288 bits to represent 128-bit UUID
        values.  Thus, where feasible, UUIDs <span class=3D"bcp14">SHOULD</=
span> be stored
        within database applications as the underlying 128-bit binary
        value.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#sectio=
n-6.13-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.13-2">For other systems, UUIDs <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY=
</span> be stored in binary
        form or as text, as appropriate.  The trade-offs to both approaches
        are as follows:<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.ht=
ml#section-6.13-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<ul class=3D"normal">
<li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.13-3.1">Storing in binary form require=
s less space and may result in faster
          data access.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.htm=
l#section-6.13-3.1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a>
</li>
          <li class=3D"normal" id=3D"section-6.13-3.2">Storing as text requ=
ires more space but may require less
          translation if the resulting text form is to be used after
          retrieval, which may make it simpler to implement.<a href=3D"http=
s://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.13-3.2" class=3D"pilcrow"=
>=C2=B6</a>
</li>
        </ul>
<p id=3D"section-6.13-4">DBMS vendors are encouraged to provide functionali=
ty to generate
        and store UUID formats defined by this specification for use as
        identifiers or left parts of identifiers such as, but not limited t=
o,
        primary keys, surrogate keys for temporal databases, foreign keys
        included in polymorphic relationships, and keys for key-value pairs=
 in
        JSON columns and key-value databases.  Applications using a monolit=
hic
        database may find using database-generated UUIDs (as opposed to
        client-generated UUIDs) provides the best UUID monotonicity.  In
        addition to UUIDs, additional identifiers <span class=3D"bcp14">MAY=
</span> be used
        to ensure integrity and feedback.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.=
org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.13-4" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-6.13-5">Designers of database schema are cautioned against=
 using name-based
        UUIDs (see Sections <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc95=
62.html#uuidv3" class=3D"auto internal xref">5.3</a> and <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#uuidv5" class=3D"auto internal xref">5=
.5</a>) as primary keys in tables.  A
        common issue observed in database schema design is the assumption t=
hat
        a particular value will never change, which later turns out to be
        an incorrect assumption.  Postal codes, license or other
        identification numbers, and numerous other such identifiers seem
        unique and unchanging at a given point time -- only later to have e=
dge
        cases where they need to change.  The subsequent change of the
        identifier, used as a "name" input for name-based UUIDs, can
        invalidate a given database structure.  In such scenarios, it is
        observed that using any non-name-based UUID version would have
        resulted in the field in question being placed somewhere that would
        have been easier to adapt to such changes (primary key excluded fro=
m
        this statement).  The general advice is to avoid name-based UUID
        natural keys and, instead, to utilize time-based UUID surrogate key=
s
        based on the aforementioned problems detailed in this section.<a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-6.13-5" class=3D"=
pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
</section>
</div>
</section>
</div>
<div id=3D"IANA">
<section id=3D"section-7">
      <h2 id=3D"name-iana-considerations">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-7" class=3D"=
section-number selfRef">7. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rf=
c9562.html#name-iana-considerations" class=3D"section-name selfRef">IANA Co=
nsiderations</a>
      </h2>
<p id=3D"section-7-1">All references to <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-e=
ditor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span>=
 in IANA registries
      (outside of those created by this document) have been replaced with
      references to this document, including the IANA URN namespace
      registration <span>[<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562=
.html#URNNamespaces" class=3D"cite xref">URNNamespaces</a>]</span> for UUID=
.  References to
      <span><a href=3D"https://rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4122#section-4.1.2" cl=
ass=3D"relref">Section 4.1.2</a> of [<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#RFC4122" class=3D"cite xref">RFC4122</a>]</span> have been
      updated to refer to <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562=
.html#format" class=3D"auto internal xref">Section 4</a> of this document.<=
a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-7-1" class=3D=
"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-7-2">Finally, IANA should track UUID Subtypes and Special =
Case "Namespace
      IDs Values" as specified in Sections <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-edito=
r.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#iana2" class=3D"auto internal xref">7.1</a> and <a h=
ref=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#iana3" class=3D"auto int=
ernal xref">7.2</a> at the
      following location: <span>&lt;<a href=3D"https://www.iana.org/assignm=
ents/uuid">https://www.iana.org/assignments/uuid</a>&gt;</span>.<a href=3D"=
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-7-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=
=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"section-7-3">When evaluating requests, the designated expert shoul=
d consider
      community feedback, how well-defined the reference specification is, =
and
      this specification's requirements.  Vendor-specific,
      application-specific, and deployment-specific values are unable to be
      registered.  Specification documents should be published in a stable,
      freely available manner (ideally, located with a URL) but need not be
      standards.  The designated expert will either approve or deny the
      registration request and communicate this decision to IANA. Denials
      should include an explanation and, if applicable, suggestions as to h=
ow
      to make the request successful.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#section-7-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<div id=3D"iana2">
<section id=3D"section-7.1">
        <h3 id=3D"name-iana-uuid-subtype-registry-">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#section-7.1" class=
=3D"section-number selfRef">7.1. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/=
rfc/rfc9562.html#name-iana-uuid-subtype-registry-" class=3D"section-name se=
lfRef">IANA UUID Subtype Registry and Registration</a>
        </h3>
<p id=3D"section-7.1-1">This specification defines the "UUID Subtypes" regi=
stry for common
        widely used UUID standards.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rf=
c/rfc9562.html#section-7.1-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
