<div id=3D"test_vectors">
<section id=3D"appendix-A">
      <h2 id=3D"name-test-vectors">
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#appendix-A" class=3D=
"section-number selfRef">Appendix A. </a><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.=
org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-test-vectors" class=3D"section-name selfRef">Test=
 Vectors</a>
      </h2>
<p id=3D"appendix-A-1">Both UUIDv1 and UUIDv6 test vectors utilize the same=
 60-bit
      timestamp: 0x1EC9414C232AB00 (138648505420000000) Tuesday, February 2=
2,
      2022 2:22:22.000000 PM GMT-05:00.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.or=
g/rfc/rfc9562.html#appendix-A-1" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<p id=3D"appendix-A-2">Both UUIDv1 and UUIDv6 utilize the same values in cl=
ock_seq and
      node; all of which have been generated with random data.  For the
      randomized node, the least significant bit of the first octet is set =
to
      a value of 1 as per <a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562=
.html#unidentifiable" class=3D"auto internal xref">Section 6.10</a>.  Thus,=
 the starting
      value 0x9E6BDECED846 was changed to 0x9F6BDECED846.<a href=3D"https:/=
/www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#appendix-A-2" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6=
</a></p>
<p id=3D"appendix-A-3">The pseudocode used for converting from a 64-bit Uni=
x timestamp to a
      100 ns Gregorian timestamp value has been left in the document for
      reference purposes.<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.=
html#appendix-A-3" class=3D"pilcrow">=C2=B6</a></p>
<span id=3D"name-test-vector-timestamp-pseud"></span><figure id=3D"figure-1=
5">
        <div class=3D"lang-pseudocode sourcecode" id=3D"appendix-A-4.1">
<pre># Gregorian-to-Unix Offset:
# The number of 100 ns intervals between the
# UUID Epoch 1582-10-15 00:00:00
# and the Unix Epoch 1970-01-01 00:00:00
# Greg_Unix_offset =3D 0x01b21dd213814000 or 122192928000000000

# Unix 64-bit Nanosecond Timestamp:
# Unix NS: Tuesday, February 22, 2022 2:22:22 PM GMT-05:00
# Unix_64_bit_ns =3D 0x16D6320C3D4DCC00 or 1645557742000000000

# Unix Nanosecond precision to Gregorian 100-nanosecond intervals
# Greg_100_ns =3D (Unix_64_bit_ns/100)+Greg_Unix_offset

# Work:
# Greg_100_ns =3D (1645557742000000000/100)+122192928000000000
# Unix_64_bit_ns =3D (138648505420000000-122192928000000000)*100

# Final:
# Greg_100_ns =3D 0x1EC9414C232AB00 or 138648505420000000
</pre>
</div>
<figcaption><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#figure-1=
5" class=3D"selfRef">Figure 15</a>:
<a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562.html#name-test-vector-tim=
estamp-pseud" class=3D"selfRef">Test Vector Timestamp Pseudocode</a>
        </figcaption></figure>
